  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and tranquil weather will prevail through this \nweekend.\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft exits generally E'ward as\na shortwave trough aloft continues to move SE'ward and \napproaches from southwestern QC, Lake Huron, and vicinity during\nthe predawn hours of this morning. The only consequence is \nstill expected to be the development of isolated to scattered \nmid/upper-level cloud cover amidst moist isentropic ascent ahead\nof the shortwave trough axis above a much drier low-level \ntropospheric column. Clear sky or breaks in cloud cover and \nlight or calm surface winds should contribute to the development\nof localized steam fog in the Black River Valley, Western \nSouthern Tier river valleys, and the lake valleys of the Finger \nLakes Region through shortly after daybreak this morning. This \nfog is expected to be shallow amidst scarce synoptic-scale low- \nlevel moisture. The fog will then dissipate by mid-morning, via \nthe onset of diurnal convective mixing of the boundary layer.\n\nThe shortwave trough aloft and attendant cold front will sweep\nSE'ward across the eastern Great Lakes and vicinity on \nThursday. The majority of the isobaric height falls aloft and \nlarge-scale forcing for ascent will be displaced well northeast \nof our area across QC, leaving a weakly-forced and moisture-starved\ncold front passage across our region. Besides the aforementioned\nincrease in mid/upper-level cloud cover ahead of the shortwave \ntrough axis aloft, a continued dry low-level tropospheric column\nis expected to permit a dry cold front passage. Behind the \nfront, and east of the axis of a ridge at the surface and aloft \nthat will approach from the Upper Great Lakes and vicinity, a \ncooler air mass will yield some increase in lake-induced \ninstability. However, the lack of synoptic-scale low-level \nmoisture will likely only support isolated to scattered lake- \neffect stratocumuli over and south of the ~16C eastern Great \nLakes this afternoon into evening and then over and generally \nsouthwest of the eastern Great Lakes overnight tonight into \nFriday morning as mean low-level flow veers from N'erly to \nNE'erly. The N'erly mean low-level flow should also yield \nisolated to scattered upslope stratocumuli generally south of \nthe eastern Great Lakes. All lake-effect and upslope stratocumuli\nare expected to be too shallow to produce precip.\n\nLate afternoon highs are expected to reach the mid 50's to \nlower 60's today. Daily lows should reach mainly the upper 20's\nto upper 30's around daybreak today and Friday. The high \npressure ridge at the surface and aloft will continue to build \nE'ward and settle over the Northeast United States and vicinity \non Friday through this weekend. Dry weather and clear to partly\ncloudy sky are expected as stabilizing subsidence accompanies \nthe ridge. Daily highs should moderate from mainly the 50's to \n60F on Friday to the mid 50's to upper 60's on Sunday as our \nregion eventually becomes located in a WAA regime along the \nwestern flank of the low-level ridge. Lows should reach mainly \nthe mid 20's to mid 30's around daybreak Saturday and mainly the\nmid 20's to upper 30's around daybreak Sunday. Note: the \nenvironment should remain favorable for localized and shallow \nsteam fog development in the Black River Valley, Western \nSouthern Tier river valleys, and the lake valleys of the Finger \nLakes Region between about 12 AM and 9 AM EDT this Friday \nthrough Sunday.",
